data.content:
Here are the three breaking changes affecting your code in EF Core 10, with fixes:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — now accepts a regular lambda, not an expression tree

Your manual expression tree construction **won't compile** because `ExecuteUpdateAsync` now takes `Func<...>` instead of `Expression<Func<...>>`.

**Fix** — replace the entire block with:
```csharp
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

---

## 2. Complex type columns are now auto-uniquified

Both `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street` previously silently shared one column. EF 10 appends a number to disambiguate, which **will generate a migration that renames columns**. Nested types (e.g. `Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City`) now use the full path (`Payment_BillingAddress_City` instead of `BillingAddress_City`).

**Fix** — pin existing column names explicitly:
```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
});

// For nested complex types, preserve old names:
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>()
    .ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment)
    .ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress)
    .Property(a => a.City)
    .HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City");
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

All methods (`CommandReaderExecuting`, `CommandReaderExecuted`, etc.) now require an additional `string logCommandText` parameter (contains redacted SQL for logging).

**Fix:**
```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)   // ← new parameter
    {
        // Use logCommandText (redacted) for logging instead of command.CommandText
    }
}
```

---

**Summary:** The biggest win is #1 — you can delete all that expression-tree plumbing and use a simple imperative lambda. For #2 and #3, add explicit column names and the new parameter respectively.
